{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2504","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2504","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2504\",\"parent_revision\":\"4de3477f4fa6f7e1180ef675d2bec22e4291019c\",\"root_revision\":\"a8f34f642972b3118e5679bdd0e0f36669b8d74279598dea57cef763b6567139\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nAs a **maintainer of the development-harness backlog system**, I want the `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/backlog/*.md` files to behave as a **read-replica cache** of the GitHub Issues backend \u2014 never a divergent local data store \u2014 so that the cache only ever lowers backend API request volume and can never hold unique, unreconciled data.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] A cache entry whose hash/`updated_at` differs from the backend is invalidated and replaced on next access.\\n- [ ] No unique data lives only in the cache \u2014 the cache holds nothing the backend lacks.\\n- [ ] Reads reflect the backend without requiring a manual `backlog_pull`.\\n- [ ] A tracked-item background sync loop exists and reconciles changesets against the backend.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **maintainer of the development-harness backlog system**, I want the `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/backlog/*.md` files to behave as a **read-replica cache** of the GitHub Issues backend \u2014 never a divergent local data store \u2014 so that the cache only ever lowers backend API request volume and can never hold unique, unreconciled data.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: PR #2496 / session investigation of the backlog cache after a direct GitHub-API body edit to #2498.\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Related**: #2498 (backlog_core decomposition) \u2014 the cache/sync code lives in the same `backlog_core` modules under review there.\\n\\n---\\n\\n_Filed via GitHub MCP fallback \u2014 the `dh:work-backlog-item` skill and backlog MCP were unavailable this session; groom with `/dh:groom-backlog-item` once the dh tooling reloads._\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-06-03)\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\n- Goal: Add hash/timestamp-based cache invalidation and a background sync loop to make `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/backlog/*.md` files a true read-replica of the GitHub Issues backend.\\\\n- Status: APPROVED-WITH-GAPS\\\\n- AVAILABLE:\\\\n  - `updated_at` timestamp is fetched from GitHub via GraphQL (`IssueNode.updatedAt`) and stored in `IssueLocalFields.updated_at` on every `_write_issue_node_to_cache` call.\\\\n  - `view_item` always live-fetches the GitHub body via `view_enrich_from_github` when the selector resolves to an issue number \u2014 it never serves the cached body for numbered selectors.\\\\n  - `backlog_list` with `from_github=True` calls `refresh_local_cache_from_github`, which runs an incremental sync keyed on `.last_sync` timestamp.\\\\n  - `_pull_if_issue_selector` is called by `update_item`, `groom_item`, and `close_item` \u2014 mutating operations already refresh the cache file before acting.\\\\n  - `sync_incremental` and `sync_full` write every received issue node to local cache via `_write_issue_node_to_cache`.\\\\n- DERIVABLE:\\\\n  - The `updated_at` value written to the cache file can be compared against the backend `updated_at` on access to detect staleness \u2014 the data already flows through, but the comparison step is absent.\\\\n  - A background-sync loop is architecturally straightforward given the existing `refresh_local_cache_from_github` primitive; the blocking point is deciding the loop host (FastMCP lifespan, OS-level cron, or per-read lazy revalidation).\\\\n- MISSING:\\\\n  - No on-access `updated_at` comparison: `backlog_list` without `from_github=True` and `view_item` calls for title-based selectors read from local files without any staleness check.\\\\n  - No background sync loop exists; reconciliation requires an explicit `backlog_pull` or `from_github=True`.\\\\n  - No content-hash or ETag tracking \u2014 only `updated_at` strings are stored locally.\\\\n  - The FastMCP lifespan hook and asyncio background-task patterns are not yet validated as host mechanisms for a sync loop.\\\\n  - Interaction between a background sync loop and concurrent MCP tool calls is not defined (write-lock / WAL strategy).\\n\\n### Artifact Classification\\n\\n- Primary artifact type: Refactor\\n- Behavioral boundary: The observable behavior of `backlog_list` (without `from_github=True`) and `backlog_view` for title-based selectors \u2014 specifically, whether they silently serve stale local data or transparently return backend-current content.\\n- Domain-semantic evaluation target: Acceptance criteria must prove that a local cache entry updated out-of-band on GitHub is automatically detected as stale and replaced before being served to a caller, without requiring the caller to pass `from_github=True`.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: Any agent or workflow that reads `backlog_list` without `from_github=True` or uses title-based `backlog_view` selectors may receive stale data after out-of-band GitHub edits.\\n- Bottleneck: The `@dh:backlog-item-groomer` agent and any orchestrator using `backlog_list` for routing decisions can act on stale item state, producing incorrect plans or duplicate work.\\n- Related: #2498 (backlog_core decomposition) \u2014 the cache/sync logic lives in the same modules being refactored there; implementing this item in parallel with #2498 risks merge conflicts.\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Cache entries will never silently hold content that differs from GitHub.\\n- Agents using `backlog_list` and `backlog_view` can trust the response without passing `from_github=True` on every call.\\n- Out-of-band GitHub edits (direct issue body updates, label changes, milestone assignments) converge automatically.\\n- Reduces the cognitive load of knowing when to pass `from_github=True`.\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen an issue body is edited directly on GitHub (out-of-band), the next `backlog_view` or `backlog_list` call for that item \u2014 without any explicit `from_github=True` flag \u2014 returns the updated content.\\n\\nSpecifically:\\n\\n1. On access, compare the cached `updated_at` against the backend value. If they differ, invalidate the local copy and re-fetch from GitHub before returning.\\n2. A background sync loop periodically reconciles all tracked items against the backend so that even items not accessed since the out-of-band edit converge.\\n3. The cache never holds unique data \u2014 no local YAML field that has no corresponding GitHub issue field.\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\n- An on-access staleness check in `backlog_list` and `backlog_view` (title-based path) that compares the cached `updated_at` against the backend value; on mismatch, calls `pull_single_issue` before serving the response.\\n- A batch-efficient variant: `batch_fetch_statuses` already fetches `updated_at` from GitHub for multiple items; the list path can use this to gate-check the whole list without N individual calls.\\n- A background sync loop (mechanism TBD \u2014 FastMCP lifespan, asyncio task, or OS cron) that calls `refresh_local_cache_from_github` on a configurable interval so even unaccessed items converge.\\n- The existing `_write_issue_node_to_cache` and `refresh_local_cache_from_github` functions are reused as the write path \u2014 no parallel write path should be introduced.\\n- A concurrency strategy for the background sync loop that is safe alongside concurrent MCP tool calls (WAL mode is already in use for SQLite; YAML file replacement strategy needs a decision).\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. Given a GitHub issue body is edited directly on GitHub (out-of-band), the next `backlog_view` call using a title-based selector for that issue returns the updated body without the caller passing any additional flags.\\n2. Given a GitHub issue body is edited directly on GitHub, the next `backlog_list` call (without `from_github=True`) includes the updated content for that item.\\n3. Given an item in the local cache whose `updated_at` matches the backend value, no additional GitHub API call is made on access (cache hit is served directly \u2014 no regression in API request volume for fresh items).\\n4. Given the MCP server is running, a background sync loop runs at a configurable interval and reconciles tracked items \u2014 observable via the `.last_sync` timestamp advancing without an explicit `backlog_list(from_github=True)` call.\\n5. Given a local cache file is deleted or corrupted, the next access creates a correct replacement from the backend without error to the caller.\\n6. Given a title-based `backlog_view` call when GitHub is unavailable, the call succeeds with the locally cached data and adds a warning that the data may be stale (graceful degradation \u2014 no regression from current behavior).\\n7. Given `batch_fetch_statuses` is available, `backlog_list` without `from_github=True` uses it to batch-check `updated_at` for all items with issue numbers rather than making N individual API calls.\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` \u2014 `refresh_local_cache_from_github`, `_sync_incremental`, `_sync_full`, `_write_issue_node_to_cache`, `pull_single_issue`, `_pull_if_issue_selector`, `view_enrich_from_github` |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/gh_client.py` \u2014 `sync_issues_graphql`, `view_enrich_from_github`, `batch_fetch_statuses`, `issue_to_local_fields` (tracks `updated_at`) |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backend_protocol.py` \u2014 `BacklogBackend` Protocol, `IssueCommentNode` (has `updated_at`), `batch_fetch_statuses` method |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/github_backend.py` \u2014 `GitHubBackend.batch_fetch_statuses`, `GitHubBackend.view_enrich_from_github` |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/github_sync.py` \u2014 `merge_item` (local metadata authoritative; section content merged) |\\n| Backlog | #2498 \u2014 backlog_core decomposition (cache/sync modules overlap; risk of merge conflict) |\\n| Backlog | #1676 \u2014 shadow device pattern / semantic ToC routing (related cache design intent) |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: #2498 (backlog_core decomposition) should be resolved or coordinated first \u2014 both items touch the same `operations.py` sync paths and a conflict mid-refactor is high-probability.\\n- Blocks: None known.\\n\\n### Blockers\\n\\n- The host mechanism for the background sync loop is unresolved: FastMCP lifespan `asyncio` task vs. OS-level cron vs. per-read lazy revalidation. Each has different concurrency implications for YAML file writes.\\n- Concurrency safety of background YAML writes alongside concurrent MCP tool calls is not defined \u2014 `_write_issue_node_to_cache` does a read-modify-write on individual files but there is no file-level lock; high-frequency background sync + concurrent writes could produce torn YAML files.\\n- Coordination with #2498 is required before implementation to avoid parallel refactors on the same modules.\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nMedium \u2014 The `updated_at` comparison hook in the read path is a contained addition (~50 LOC in `operations.py`). The background sync loop is the higher-uncertainty piece: the FastMCP lifespan integration and YAML write-safety strategy need a spike before sizing confidently. Total estimate: 3-5 focused implementation sessions.\\n\\n### Problem (WHAT)\\n\\nThe `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/backlog/*.md` files are intended as a cache to reduce GitHub API requests. Today they can diverge silently from the backend with no automatic reconciliation path.\\n\\nCurrent read/write path baseline (traced from source):\\n\\n**`backlog_view` (numbered selector):** Always live-fetches the GitHub issue body via `view_enrich_from_github` \u2192 `_fetch_issue_graphql`. The live body is used for `body` and `sections`. The local YAML is used only for groomed date. This path is effectively cache-coherent already for numbered selectors.\\n\\n**`backlog_view` (title-based selector):** Calls `find_item(parse_backlog(), selector)` \u2014 reads from local YAML/MD files. Falls back to `ItemNotFoundError` if not found locally. Does not contact GitHub. Serves potentially stale local data.\\n\\n**`backlog_list` without `from_github=True`:** Reads local cache files only. The `updated_at` field is stored in local YAML frontmatter but is never compared to the backend value before serving the response. A directly-edited GitHub issue body appears stale in list results until the next explicit pull.\\n\\n**`backlog_list` with `from_github=True`:** Calls `refresh_local_cache_from_github`, which runs an incremental or full sync using `.last_sync` timestamp file. Writes `_write_issue_node_to_cache` for each received issue node. This path is coherent but requires an explicit caller opt-in.\\n\\n**Mutating ops (`update_item`, `groom_item`, `close_item`):** Call `_pull_if_issue_selector` before operating \u2014 these always pull from GitHub first when the selector is numeric. Coherent.\\n\\n**What is missing:** No on-access staleness check using `updated_at`. No content-hash or ETag. No background sync loop that periodically validates tracked items without caller opt-in.\\n\\n### Why\\n\\nSurfaced during PR #2496: a direct GitHub-API edit to issue #2498's body left the local cache unreconciled. `backlog_list` (without `from_github=True`) served the stale local copy. No auto-invalidation path existed.\\n\\nA grep of `backlog_core` (non-test) confirmed: `updated_at` is tracked in local frontmatter but no comparison against the live backend value exists in the read path.\\n\\n## Why\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nSurfaced during PR #2496: a direct GitHub-API edit to issue #2498's body left the local cache unreconciled with no auto-invalidation path. A grep of `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core` (non-test) found per-item `updated_at` timestamps are tracked, but **no content-hash/etag-based cache-invalidation logic** was found.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Problem What\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/backlog/*.md` files exist to reduce backend API requests (a cache). Today a copy in that cache can diverge from the backend with no automatic reconciliation path. A cache whose contents are never validated against the source of truth is a second data store, not a cache.\\n\\nThe backlog MCP server should:\\n\\n1. **Track** every backend item read or written through it.\\n2. **Validate on access** \u2014 on each read/write, compare the cached copy's write-timestamp (and/or content-hash) against the backend; on mismatch, **invalidate and replace** the cached copy from the backend (the source of truth).\\n3. **Background sync loop** \u2014 periodically check timestamp/hash for tracked items and sync the changeset, i.e. read-replica-DB semantics, so out-of-band backend edits converge without a manual pull.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Grooming Prerequisite Resolve Before Designing\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nTrace the actual read/write paths to establish current behavior first:\\n\\n- `backlog_view` / `backlog_list` read path \u2014 live-fetch vs cache-read vs cache-then-validate?\\n- `backlog_pull` / `backlog_sync` write/reconcile path \u2014 what triggers a refresh, and is there any `updated_at`/hash comparison today?\\n\\nEstablish the baseline, then design the invalidation + background-sync mechanism.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Problem (What)\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/backlog/*.md` files exist to reduce backend API requests (a cache). Today a copy in that cache can diverge from the backend with no automatic reconciliation path. A cache whose contents are never validated against the source of truth is a second data store, not a cache.\\n\\nThe backlog MCP server should:\\n\\n1. **Track** every backend item read or written through it.\\n2. **Validate on access** \u2014 on each read/write, compare the cached copy's write-timestamp (and/or content-hash) against the backend; on mismatch, **invalidate and replace** the cached copy from the backend (the source of truth).\\n3. **Background sync loop** \u2014 periodically check timestamp/hash for tracked items and sync the changeset, i.e. read-replica-DB semantics, so out-of-band backend edits converge without a manual pull.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Grooming Prerequisite (Resolve Before Designing)\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nTrace the actual read/write paths to establish current behavior first:\\n\\n- `backlog_view` / `backlog_list` read path \u2014 live-fetch vs cache-read vs cache-then-validate?\\n- `backlog_pull` / `backlog_sync` write/reconcile path \u2014 what triggers a refresh, and is there any `updated_at`/hash comparison today?\\n\\nEstablish the baseline, then design the invalidation + background-sync mechanism.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"a5550b1567debbf8707f78b8e64d21782f96f793b786469f308871252dab854a\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPtZh2Q\"}"}